1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,796
Monday morning. February 17th, 2003.

2
00:00:05,346 --> 00:00:09,214
Two floors beneath the
streets of Antwerp, Belgium,

3
00:00:09,254 --> 00:00:13,626
staff arrive at one of the most
heavily protected vaults in the world.

4
00:00:13,756 --> 00:00:16,109
The vault door is standing open.

5
00:00:17,159 --> 00:00:22,097
Inside, rows of steel safe-deposit
boxes have been forced apart.

6
00:00:22,227 --> 00:00:27,439
The floor is buried in paper, torn
envelopes, and empty jewelry cases.

7
00:00:28,139 --> 00:00:34,224
Diamonds, gold, and jewelry — worth an estimated
one hundred million dollars — are gone.

8
00:00:35,274 --> 00:00:39,017
There was no explosion.
No alarm. No witnesses.

9
00:00:39,147 --> 00:00:44,105
A vault reportedly protected by ten
layers of security had not been smashed.

10
00:00:44,145 --> 00:00:48,300
It had been beaten —
quietly, one layer at a time.

11
00:00:49,350 --> 00:00:54,406
This is the story of how it was done, the
mistake that brought the thieves down,

12
00:00:54,446 --> 00:00:58,719
and the question that remains
unanswered more than twenty years later.

13
00:00:58,849 --> 00:01:00,654
Where are the diamonds?

14
00:01:05,554 --> 00:01:09,724
To understand this crime, you
first have to understand the city.

15
00:01:09,854 --> 00:01:14,057
Antwerp sits in the north of
Belgium, on the river Scheldt,

16
00:01:14,097 --> 00:01:16,963
about forty-five kilometers from Brussels.

17
00:01:17,093 --> 00:01:22,726
For more than five hundred years, its fortunes
have been tied to one thing above all else.

18
00:01:22,976 --> 00:01:24,242
Diamonds.

19
00:01:25,292 --> 00:01:30,729
By the early two thousands, Antwerp was
the center of the global diamond trade.

20
00:01:30,859 --> 00:01:35,954
By widely cited estimates, roughly eighty
percent of the world's rough diamonds

21
00:01:35,994 --> 00:01:38,002
passed through this one city.

22
00:01:38,482 --> 00:01:43,169
Most of that trade happens inside a
district of just a few streets beside

23
00:01:43,209 --> 00:01:45,241
Antwerp's Central Station.

24
00:01:45,371 --> 00:01:48,580
Hoveniersstraat. Schupstraat. Rijfstraat.

25
00:01:48,930 --> 00:01:53,788
Traders of dozens of nationalities
work side by side in these few blocks —

26
00:01:53,828 --> 00:01:57,370
rough stones arriving from
mines around the world,

27
00:01:57,410 --> 00:02:00,804
polished stones leaving
for jewelers everywhere.

28
00:02:00,934 --> 00:02:04,571
Here, fortunes in stones
change hands every day —

29
00:02:04,611 --> 00:02:09,810
and deals are traditionally sealed with
a handshake and a single word: mazal.

30
00:02:10,290 --> 00:02:14,384
The district is watched by
cameras, patrolled by police,

31
00:02:14,424 --> 00:02:17,757
and protected by barriers
against vehicle traffic.

32
00:02:18,357 --> 00:02:24,097
And at its heart, at Schupstraat 9 to
11, stood the Antwerp Diamond Centre.

33
00:02:24,227 --> 00:02:28,840
Its tenants were diamond dealers.
And two floors below street level,

34
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:33,935
behind a massive steel door, was the
vault where many of them kept their most

35
00:02:33,975 --> 00:02:35,259
valuable goods.

36
00:02:35,389 --> 00:02:40,384
To the people who trusted it, that vault
was about as safe as a place could be.

37
00:02:44,834 --> 00:02:49,638
Around two years before the robbery,
a new tenant moved into the building.

38
00:02:49,768 --> 00:02:53,767
He was Italian. He presented
himself as a diamond dealer.

39
00:02:53,807 --> 00:02:58,267
And he rented an office inside
the Antwerp Diamond Centre.

40
00:02:58,747 --> 00:03:01,948
His name was Leonardo Notarbartolo.

41
00:03:02,298 --> 00:03:07,175
As a tenant, he had what every
legitimate dealer in the building had:

42
00:03:07,215 --> 00:03:12,960
an office, a safe-deposit box of his own,
and a reason to walk into the vault area.

43
00:03:13,090 --> 00:03:17,255
To the guards and the other
dealers, he was a familiar face.

44
00:03:17,295 --> 00:03:21,361
Polite. Unremarkable. The kind
of man nobody looks at twice.

45
00:03:21,491 --> 00:03:26,465
A Belgian court would later conclude
that he was something else entirely:

46
00:03:26,505 --> 00:03:28,893
the man who organized the robbery.

47
00:03:29,943 --> 00:03:32,758
Notarbartolo did not work alone.

48
00:03:32,888 --> 00:03:36,579
He was connected to a group
of thieves based around Turin,

49
00:03:36,619 --> 00:03:41,360
in northern Italy — a crew the press
would later call the School of Turin.

50
00:03:42,060 --> 00:03:47,115
The men convicted alongside him became
known by nicknames that sound as if they

51
00:03:47,155 --> 00:03:49,002
were written for a movie.

52
00:03:49,132 --> 00:03:55,602
Elio D'Onorio, called the Genius — described
as an expert in alarms and electronics.

53
00:03:55,732 --> 00:04:00,198
Ferdinando Finotto, the Monster
— described as a lock specialist,

54
00:04:00,238 --> 00:04:03,366
a skilled driver, and a
man of enormous strength.

55
00:04:03,496 --> 00:04:09,606
Pietro Tavano, known as Speedy — described
as Notarbartolo's longtime friend.

56
00:04:09,736 --> 00:04:15,095
And a fifth man, an older specialist
known only as the King of Keys,

57
00:04:15,135 --> 00:04:21,276
said to be one of the finest key forgers
alive. His identity has never been established.

58
00:04:22,326 --> 00:04:24,808
One warning before we go further.

59
00:04:24,958 --> 00:04:30,204
Much of what is known about these men
— including their nicknames and roles —

60
00:04:30,244 --> 00:04:33,922
comes from Notarbartolo's own
later account of the heist.

61
00:04:34,052 --> 00:04:38,297
And as we'll see, his version
of events is not the only one.

62
00:04:42,747 --> 00:04:46,017
Breaking into the vault meant
defeating a system that,

63
00:04:46,057 --> 00:04:48,343
on paper, looked overwhelming.

64
00:04:48,473 --> 00:04:51,485
Accounts of the heist
describe it in layers.

65
00:04:51,615 --> 00:04:56,038
A combination dial with one
hundred million possible codes.

66
00:04:56,168 --> 00:05:01,093
A massive steel door, opened with
a key reportedly about a foot long.

67
00:05:01,223 --> 00:05:05,831
Infrared heat detectors. A seismic
sensor to pick up vibration.

68
00:05:05,871 --> 00:05:11,620
Doppler radar. A light sensor. And a
magnetic field that would trigger the alarm

69
00:05:11,660 --> 00:05:13,576
if the door was opened.

70
00:05:13,706 --> 00:05:17,847
Inside, every safe-deposit
box had a lock of its own.

71
00:05:17,977 --> 00:05:23,231
And beyond all of that: guards, cameras,
and the police presence of the diamond

72
00:05:23,271 --> 00:05:24,490
district itself.

73
00:05:25,540 --> 00:05:29,277
For a thief, the problem is not
only how to beat each layer.

74
00:05:29,317 --> 00:05:34,912
It is how to learn what the layers are —
without anyone noticing that you are looking.

75
00:05:35,962 --> 00:05:40,726
According to Notarbartolo's later
account, the answer was patience.

76
00:05:40,856 --> 00:05:44,481
As a tenant, he could go down
to the vault again and again,

77
00:05:44,521 --> 00:05:48,624
like any other dealer — and he said
he spent many months studying it.

78
00:05:48,754 --> 00:05:55,085
He claimed he secretly photographed the vault,
using a tiny camera concealed inside a pen.

79
00:05:55,785 --> 00:05:59,172
In his telling, one step
mattered more than any other.

80
00:05:59,302 --> 00:06:03,747
He said the crew hid a small camera
of their own above the vault door —

81
00:06:03,787 --> 00:06:07,942
positioned to record the guards
as they dialed the combination.

82
00:06:08,072 --> 00:06:13,170
If that account is true, the vault's
first line of defense was defeated without

83
00:06:13,210 --> 00:06:14,690
anyone touching it.

84
00:06:15,740 --> 00:06:20,951
He also described a rehearsal: the crew
practicing on a replica of the vault's

85
00:06:20,991 --> 00:06:26,342
defenses, until every step could be
performed quickly and in near silence.

86
00:06:26,472 --> 00:06:32,034
Belgian investigators have never
confirmed every detail of this version.

87
00:06:32,164 --> 00:06:37,660
What is not in doubt is that whoever entered
that vault knew exactly what they would face.

88
00:06:38,710 --> 00:06:42,184
By February 2003, the plan was set.

89
00:06:42,314 --> 00:06:47,048
The target was a weekend — two days
when the offices would be closed and the

90
00:06:47,088 --> 00:06:49,404
building would be at its quietest.

91
00:06:53,854 --> 00:06:58,564
According to the account Notarbartolo
later gave to WIRED magazine,

92
00:06:58,604 --> 00:07:02,861
the crew did not walk in through
the front entrance on the street.

93
00:07:02,991 --> 00:07:06,264
They approached from a
neighboring property at the back —

94
00:07:06,304 --> 00:07:08,946
away from the cameras
that watched the street.

95
00:07:09,646 --> 00:07:12,433
Every defense, he claimed, had a weakness.

96
00:07:12,563 --> 00:07:17,957
The heat sensor, the story goes, was
neutralized with something as ordinary as

97
00:07:17,997 --> 00:07:22,411
hairspray — a thin coating that
briefly insulated the detector.

98
00:07:22,541 --> 00:07:25,148
The light sensor was covered with tape.

99
00:07:25,278 --> 00:07:29,942
And the magnetic sensor on the vault
door — the alarm that should have sounded

100
00:07:29,982 --> 00:07:34,647
the moment the door moved — was reportedly
bypassed by holding its two halves

101
00:07:34,687 --> 00:07:38,700
together with an aluminum plate,
so the circuit never broke.

102
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:40,945
And the famous key?

103
00:07:41,075 --> 00:07:45,787
In Notarbartolo's account, the crew
never needed to forge it at all.

104
00:07:45,917 --> 00:07:49,403
He said the real key was kept
close to the vault itself —

105
00:07:49,443 --> 00:07:52,182
where anyone who knew
to look could find it.

106
00:07:53,232 --> 00:07:58,737
If that is true, one of the most sophisticated
vaults in the world was undone by

107
00:07:58,777 --> 00:08:00,433
a simple human habit.

108
00:08:00,783 --> 00:08:04,575
It is a claim that has never
been independently proven.

109
00:08:04,615 --> 00:08:07,780
But what happened next is beyond dispute.

110
00:08:12,230 --> 00:08:18,435
The break-in took place over the
weekend of February 15th and 16th, 2003.

111
00:08:18,565 --> 00:08:22,674
By Monday morning, it would be
discovered. By the following Friday,

112
00:08:22,714 --> 00:08:24,264
there would be an arrest.

113
00:08:24,964 --> 00:08:28,052
That weekend, the diamond
district fell quiet.

114
00:08:28,182 --> 00:08:31,977
The trading halls were
empty. The offices were dark.

115
00:08:32,677 --> 00:08:36,527
Two levels below the street, the
thieves reached the vault door.

116
00:08:36,657 --> 00:08:42,066
With the combination known, the key
in hand, and the sensors neutralized,

117
00:08:42,106 --> 00:08:45,988
the door that was supposed to
stop everyone simply swung open.

118
00:08:47,038 --> 00:08:52,669
Inside were rows of safe-deposit boxes
— and the crew began forcing them open,

119
00:08:52,709 --> 00:08:55,228
one after another, for hours.

120
00:08:55,928 --> 00:08:58,311
How many boxes did they open?

121
00:08:58,441 --> 00:09:00,594
Even that is disputed.

122
00:09:01,194 --> 00:09:05,888
Commonly cited figures range from one
hundred and nine to one hundred and

123
00:09:05,928 --> 00:09:11,232
twenty-three boxes — out of a total
variously reported at between one hundred

124
00:09:11,272 --> 00:09:14,194
and sixty and one hundred and eighty-nine.

125
00:09:14,674 --> 00:09:18,349
What they took is just as
uncertain. Loose diamonds.

126
00:09:18,389 --> 00:09:20,886
Gold. Jewelry. Other valuables.

127
00:09:21,016 --> 00:09:24,928
Much of what was in those
boxes was never documented.

128
00:09:25,058 --> 00:09:29,159
In the diamond trade, discretion
is part of the business —

129
00:09:29,199 --> 00:09:33,379
which makes the true size of the
loss almost impossible to measure.

130
00:09:34,429 --> 00:09:39,508
Before Monday morning, the crew was
gone, leaving behind a vault in chaos.

131
00:09:39,638 --> 00:09:44,259
And somewhere between Antwerp and Brussels,
they made a decision that would cost

132
00:09:44,299 --> 00:09:45,494
them everything.

133
00:09:50,194 --> 00:09:55,099
On the morning of Monday, February
17th, the robbery was discovered.

134
00:09:55,229 --> 00:10:00,741
The vault was in chaos: box doors hanging
open, paper and envelopes scattered

135
00:10:00,781 --> 00:10:02,127
across the floor.

136
00:10:02,257 --> 00:10:07,712
Antwerp police — including the specialized
unit dedicated to the diamond trade,

137
00:10:07,752 --> 00:10:10,854
known as the Diamond
Squad — were called in.

138
00:10:11,554 --> 00:10:16,727
At first, investigators faced a
crime with almost no starting point.

139
00:10:16,767 --> 00:10:22,396
The vault door had not been blasted or
cut. There was no obvious trail to follow.

140
00:10:23,446 --> 00:10:28,098
But that same morning, some
thirty-five kilometers to the south,

141
00:10:28,138 --> 00:10:33,007
something unusual turned up in a patch
of woodland beside the E19 highway,

142
00:10:33,047 --> 00:10:36,113
near Vilvoorde, just outside Brussels.

143
00:10:36,593 --> 00:10:41,001
The land was looked after by a
local man named August Van Camp.

144
00:10:41,131 --> 00:10:46,600
And on his property, he found a pile of rubbish
that someone had dumped among the trees.

145
00:10:47,300 --> 00:10:52,507
Among the garbage were envelopes bearing
the name of the Antwerp Diamond Centre.

146
00:10:52,637 --> 00:10:54,762
Van Camp called the police.

147
00:10:59,212 --> 00:11:04,453
What investigators recovered from those
woods would become the core of the case.

148
00:11:04,583 --> 00:11:08,359
There were torn envelopes
connected to the diamond centre.

149
00:11:08,489 --> 00:11:12,763
There were documents and other items that
could be traced to the people who had

150
00:11:12,803 --> 00:11:14,034
thrown them away.

151
00:11:14,164 --> 00:11:17,310
And there was a half-eaten
salami sandwich.

152
00:11:18,360 --> 00:11:23,682
The sandwich carried DNA. Investigators
tied it to a member of the crew —

153
00:11:23,722 --> 00:11:27,685
although accounts differ on
exactly whose DNA it was.

154
00:11:27,815 --> 00:11:32,351
Other items pointed directly
to Leonardo Notarbartolo.

155
00:11:33,401 --> 00:11:38,533
It was a staggering contrast. A crew
that had allegedly outwitted one of the

156
00:11:38,573 --> 00:11:44,242
most secure vaults on the planet had been
exposed by a pile of trash in the woods.

157
00:11:45,292 --> 00:11:50,032
Notarbartolo later described the
dumping as a panicked mistake.

158
00:11:50,162 --> 00:11:55,438
In his account, Speedy — nervous and
exhausted — scattered the garbage in the

159
00:11:55,478 --> 00:11:58,253
woods instead of getting
rid of it as planned.

160
00:11:58,383 --> 00:12:03,587
Whatever the reason, it handed
investigators a direct line to the suspects.

161
00:12:08,037 --> 00:12:13,385
On February 21st, 2003 — just
five days after the break-in —

162
00:12:13,425 --> 00:12:17,547
Notarbartolo returned to
the Antwerp Diamond Centre.

163
00:12:17,677 --> 00:12:21,094
Detectives were waiting
for him. He was arrested.

164
00:12:21,444 --> 00:12:24,999
From there, investigators
turned to the men around him.

165
00:12:26,049 --> 00:12:32,247
In 2005, a Belgian court convicted
Notarbartolo of organizing the heist and

166
00:12:32,287 --> 00:12:35,300
sentenced him to ten years in prison.

167
00:12:35,430 --> 00:12:41,798
Elio D'Onorio, Ferdinando Finotto, and
Pietro Tavano each received five years.

168
00:12:41,928 --> 00:12:44,997
The King of Keys was never identified.

169
00:12:46,047 --> 00:12:52,298
Then, in 2009, Notarbartolo told his
side of the story to WIRED magazine.

170
00:12:52,338 --> 00:12:54,715
And it reframed everything.

171
00:12:54,845 --> 00:12:58,606
He claimed the crew had been
hired by a diamond merchant.

172
00:12:58,736 --> 00:13:02,574
He claimed the robbery was part
of an insurance fraud scheme.

173
00:13:02,704 --> 00:13:06,183
And he claimed the haul was
far smaller than reported —

174
00:13:06,223 --> 00:13:10,750
around eighteen million euros, or
roughly twenty million dollars.

175
00:13:11,230 --> 00:13:16,149
Many have doubted his story. Belgian
authorities have never confirmed it,

176
00:13:16,189 --> 00:13:21,981
and the merchant he described has never been
identified to the satisfaction of the courts.

177
00:13:22,111 --> 00:13:25,752
He also had every reason to
minimize what was stolen.

178
00:13:25,882 --> 00:13:29,499
And yet his version is hard
to dismiss completely —

179
00:13:29,539 --> 00:13:34,743
because no one has ever been able to
prove exactly what was inside those boxes.

180
00:13:39,193 --> 00:13:43,652
Notarbartolo was released
on parole in 2009.

181
00:13:43,782 --> 00:13:48,628
And the diamonds? The overwhelming
majority have never been recovered.

182
00:13:49,328 --> 00:13:53,623
Diamonds are small, valuable,
and difficult to trace.

183
00:13:53,663 --> 00:13:58,887
Once they are recut or quietly sold,
a stone can vanish into the legitimate

184
00:13:58,927 --> 00:14:01,189
market almost without a trace.

185
00:14:01,669 --> 00:14:05,022
Was it one hundred million
dollars? Or twenty?

186
00:14:05,372 --> 00:14:08,760
A brilliant theft? Or a
fraud dressed up as one?

187
00:14:09,110 --> 00:14:11,027
Who was the King of Keys?

188
00:14:11,377 --> 00:14:14,287
And who, if anyone, stood behind the crew?

189
00:14:15,337 --> 00:14:21,223
Leonardo Notarbartolo has told his story.
The courts have delivered their verdict.

190
00:14:21,353 --> 00:14:24,816
But neither has answered the
question that matters most.

191
00:14:25,366 --> 00:14:30,441
More than two decades later, Guinness
World Records still lists the Antwerp

192
00:14:30,481 --> 00:14:34,676
Diamond Centre robbery as the
largest diamond heist in history.

193
00:14:34,806 --> 00:14:39,209
It has inspired books, documentaries,
and endless speculation.

194
00:14:39,339 --> 00:14:43,289
But the most important part of
the story is still locked away —

195
00:14:43,329 --> 00:14:48,548
not in a vault, but in the silence of the
people who know where the diamonds went.

196
00:14:50,248 --> 00:14:55,657
This has been WAR FILES. We don't
tell stories — we open the files.

197
00:14:56,207 --> 00:14:58,774
If you want the next one, subscribe.

